<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  The sandbox is not optional here. ExtensionFoundation builds the XPC
	  connection for a preview request out of the extension's container
	  identity, and an unsandboxed appex has none, so Quick Look crashes on a
	  nil dictionary key before our code ever runs. Every preview extension
	  Apple ships carries these same two entitlements.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  WKWebView runs its content and networking work in separate XPC services.
	  Inside a sandboxed extension without this, those services fail to launch:

	      WebProcessProxy::didFinishLaunching: Invalid connection identifier
	      Could not signal service com.apple.WebKit.Networking: 113

	  The web view then stays blank forever and never calls didFinish, so the
	  preview panel is empty even though the HTML was generated correctly. This
	  is needed even though the page is loaded from memory and loads nothing
	  over the network.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  Local images beside the document are NOT readable with this set of
	  entitlements. Quick Look grants access to the previewed file alone, so
	  ![](diagram.png) falls back to its alt text. Remote https images work.

	  The fix is a read only temporary exception:

	      com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-only
	      with the value /Users/ (and /Volumes/ for external disks)

	  That is a restricted entitlement. Adding it to an ad hoc signed build does
	  not merely fail to grant access, it gets the extension killed the moment it
	  touches a sibling file, with no error logged and no crash report written.
	  It is only honoured when the signature is backed by a real identity, so add
	  it together with Developer ID signing, never before.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
